\section*{APPENDIX A: NATURE OF THE REFERENCE DATA STRUCTURE}

The reference data contain cluster structures with a large variety of sizes and shapes. As an example, we describe the nature of the structures of the clusters containing 16 atoms. The structures can be classified as regular and irregular. The regular structures include planar shapes, rods, ladders, and cubes, as well as a mixture of all these geometries. In the irregular structures, atoms are randomly distributed with the only constraint that bond lengths are neither too short nor too long. Ten selected 16 -atom NaCl cluster structures, found in the reference data, are illustrated in Fig. 4. Other cluster sizes contain similar types of structures; however, the diversity of geometries grows as the number of atoms in the cluster increases.

\section*{APPENDIX B: ATOMIC FORCES}

The calculation of atomic forces is straightforward. The force exerted on atom \(j\) is given by
\[
\mathbf{F}_{j}=-\frac{\partial U_{\mathrm{tot}}}{\partial \mathbf{r}_{j}}-\sum_{k=1}^{N} \frac{\partial U_{\mathrm{tot}}}{\partial q_{k}} \frac{\partial q_{k}}{\mathbf{r}_{j}},
\]
where the second term vanishes as required by Eq. (3). So we do not have to calculate the derivative of the charges \(q_{i}\) with

\begin{figure}
\includegraphics[alt={},max width=\textwidth]{https://cdn.mathpix.com/cropped/a7b21cff-47e5-4ad5-8401-e72c55f5ea4f-5.jpg?height=1217&width=869&top_left_y=241&top_left_x=1074}
\captionsetup{labelformat=empty}
\caption{FIG. 4. (Color online) Illustration of structural diversity in the reference data sets for the case of 16-atom clusters: (a) ladder-type structure, (b) ladder-type structure which is bent, (c) cuboid structure, (d) cuboid structure in which three bonds are broken, (e) a nearly elongated random structure, (f) a ladder structure attached to a cuboid, (g) two cuboid structures which share a cube, (h) a rodlike structure attached to a plane, (i) a compact random structure, and (j) another compact random structure.}
\end{figure}
respect to the atomic positions. This is analogous to the use of the Hellmann-Feynman theorem in standard DFT, which tells us that the derivative of the wave function (and consequently also of the charge density) with respect to the atomic positions is not required. So the formula simplifies to
\[
\mathbf{F}_{j}=-\sum_{i=1}^{N}\left(q_{i} \frac{\partial \chi_{i}}{\partial \mathbf{r}_{j}}\right)+\sum_{i>j}^{N} q_{i} q_{j} \frac{\partial V_{i j}}{\partial \mathbf{r}_{j}},
\]
where \(V_{i j}=\frac{\operatorname{erf}\left(\gamma_{i j} r_{i j}\right)}{r_{i j}}\). The derivatives of \(\chi_{i}\) with respect to atomic positions are calculated in the neural-network process. The first term describes the force acting on atom \(j\) arising from the local charge distribution around this atom. The second term in the force expression is the contribution arising from the electrostatic energy of the global charge distribution excluding the self-energy of a Gaussian.
